## 2. Generic badge format

```markdown
[![ALT TEXT](https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/actions/workflows/WORKFLOW_FILE/badge.svg)](https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/actions/workflows/WORKFLOW_FILE)
```

- `OWNER` — GitHub user or organization
- `REPO` — repository name
- `WORKFLOW_FILE` — filename in `.github/workflows/`, e.g. `ci.yml`

## 3. Optional query parameters

GitHub’s native badge supports:

- `?branch=main` — show status for a specific branch
- `?event=push` — show status for a specific event
- Combine with `&`

Example:

```markdown
[![CI](https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg?branch=main&event=push)](https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
```

## 4. Using the workflow name instead of filename

Spaces must be URL-encoded as `%20`:

```markdown
[![CI](https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/actions/workflows/CI%20Build/badge.svg)](https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/actions/workflows/CI%20Build)
```

The filename is usually more reliable.

